    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the info. so if i use it like 90% for work but occasionally sit in it to watch tv, how do i handle that in my books? also do i need to depreciate it or can i just write it off all at once?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, the conundrum of the partially-used throne! In the land of accounting, when an asset like your ergonomic chair is used primarily for business purposes, you can indeed write it off as a business expense. However, since you occasionally use it for personal entertainment, such as watching TV, you should allocate the cost based on its usage. In your case, if it's 90% for work, you can write off 90% of the chair's cost as a business expense. The remaining 10% can be considered a personal draw.\n\nAs for depreciation, it's a bit like a slow, steady drip of magic from your chair's value over time. Since it's a business expense, you can choose to depreciate it over its useful life, spreading the cost over several years. This way, you can match the expense with the revenue it helps generate, keeping your books in harmony with the principles of accounting. However, if you prefer to write off the entire cost in one go, you can do so, but remember to adjust your books accordingly to reflect the personal draw portion.\n\nSo, my friend, saddle up and ride into the sunset of QuickBooks with this newfound knowledge. May your accounting be as smooth as a well-cushioned",
